<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isaac Newton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1643">1643</a>–<a href="/years/1727">1727</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isaac Newton was an English mathematician and physicist, one of the central figures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution</a>. In his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a> of 1687 he set out the laws of motion and universal gravitation, and he also did fundamental work in optics and, independently of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a>, developed calculus. He was president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> from 1703 and Master of the Royal Mint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isaac-newton.jpg" alt="Isaac Newton" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Godfrey Kneller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Sir_Isaac_Newton,_1689_(brightened).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the seventeenth century England became a centre of scientific inquiry: the Royal Society was founded in 1660, <b>Isaac Newton</b> published his Principia in 1687, and Thomas Hobbes and John Locke laid foundations of modern political thought.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Émilie du Châtelet finished her French translation of <b>Isaac Newton</b>’s Principia, with a long commentary of her own, in 1749, the year she died; published complete in 1759, it is still the standard French version.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1687 <b>Isaac Newton</b>’s Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy, the Principia, showed that the same three laws of motion and a single force of universal gravitation explained the fall of bodies on Earth, the orbits of the Moon and planets, the tides and the paths of comets.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Such near-simultaneous firsts could become quarrels: <b>Isaac Newton</b> derived the results of calculus in 1665–1666, but Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz published first, in 1684, and although they reached them independently the credit was bitterly disputed.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-invented-it-first">Who Invented It First?</a></li></ul></section>
